{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>If you will but hear me</b> — its parsing is: אם אתה אם תשמעני (if you, if you hear me), and its meaning is like 'if you will hear me.' The doubling comes to press the matter, as in 'Turn aside, my lord, turn aside to me' (Judges 4:18), 'Are you good, are you good?' (Judges 11:25 — i.e. 'Are you truly good?'), and likewise 'and to say, to say concerning it' (II Chronicles 32:17), and likewise 'and I — where am I going?' (Genesis 37:30), 'and I turned, I' (Ecclesiastes 2:12), 'and I saw, I' (Ecclesiastes 2:13), 'for all the congregation, all of them, are holy' (Numbers 16:3) — all of them pursue the same meaning (i.e. intensification through repetition). And so in my opinion: 'from a thread to a sandal-strap, and if I take' (Genesis 14:23) — its parsing is: from a thread to a sandal-strap I will take from all that is yours. Or it may mean: 'if you' in the sense of 'as you said' — that is, your mouth and your heart are equal in the matter, 'and if you will hear me.' And likewise 'and their brothers said to them, What are you?' (Judges 18:8). And similar to this expression, in my opinion, is 'Why am I thus?' (Genesis 25:22). Perhaps this is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 23:13), who rendered 'אם את עבד לי טיבו' — that you will do my will as you said."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The verse citations from Ecclesiastes are given by Ramban as קהלת ב יב יג, which are consecutive verses 2:12 and 2:13 rendered as listed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:33:33.903911133Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}